[{:db/id "q-338404/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "0b5cfed7-5fb5-5f34-8a2a-2258069e7061" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338404/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "75721307-a0a5-5c60-97d9-f2a1a5a6ed29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338404/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-338404/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338404" :question/id #uuid "dc70fedc-2c3c-5334-9413-78a7c2f33957" :question/math-academy-id "q-338404" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x)}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(3x+5)=3(1)+5=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule (continuity) in the denominator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x) & =\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}+2(1)+5}+2(1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{1+2+5}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x+5}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+2x+5}+2x} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-338404/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1bddb74-97be-5f35-b559-afb707e6e964"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338404"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-71142"] :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =-2y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-51841"] :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =1+0 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-86328/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "a7e40331-f8f6-5b35-bb34-2cf41b5efe7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86328/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "cd996b13-7796-55ba-890e-b7acf2cf78fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86328/selection/4bd87ad5cdff0b2d0aa7574d5c7450ac33fa34774f895ce16d251ca09a1932ec" :answer/id #uuid "b90d25f3-4baf-58b7-ba76-959ff7f39d33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86328/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "174c89ad-c596-549c-be65-dea6cf4cbf97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86328/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "b9031b94-926d-5f93-848a-4e524bbb0eb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86328/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc925353-254d-59c1-84b4-1fc5165b70fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-86328/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-86328/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-86328/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-86328/selection/4bd87ad5cdff0b2d0aa7574d5c7450ac33fa34774f895ce16d251ca09a1932ec" "q-86328/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-86328/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-86328" :question/id #uuid "284d9cbf-5f0f-5fe0-8b82-9cf1281e3bcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-86328" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{1}{-\\frac{1}{2}}=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-86328/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecd367ac-2a68-56d0-b553-85277a9c921d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86328"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-69924"] :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}÷\\frac{b}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{5{b}^{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{b} & = \\\\ \\frac{35{b}^{2}}{2b} & = \\\\ \\frac{35{b}^{\\cancel{2}}}{2\\cancel{b}} & = \\\\ \\frac{35b}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $b$ in the numerator and denominator." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-262211"] :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(3,5,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(4,3,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(4-3)^{2}+(3-5)^{2}+(1-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-288816/selection/21bc1f5ac2b6db8ca11973d9b85c1eeb6af2cd920b556e0cf3d98ab9a728286a" :answer/id #uuid "3de3be6d-b395-5d09-bbab-10dfdc21ffb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-288816/selection/b2b2487c52633be6396d294ebadec1265f7827ec4e8b5458042006050873a19a" :answer/id #uuid "57bba0ba-0886-5ff7-9dbe-f633d561ea32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-288816/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "f6fde9e0-3b3e-5a8a-8414-2ccd2c3ac7c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-288816/selection/3de79bddf6f841c5881cdae443d3aaa683eb5842e99646c5b7e2837e89382703" :answer/id #uuid "424f0306-7c2f-54b9-bf17-95e7ad25a8e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-288816/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "7faa68bf-704a-519a-a436-11963299936c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-288816/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f53737ff-b1fd-54c6-926c-e2c069766dff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-288816/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-288816/selection/21bc1f5ac2b6db8ca11973d9b85c1eeb6af2cd920b556e0cf3d98ab9a728286a" "q-288816/selection/b2b2487c52633be6396d294ebadec1265f7827ec4e8b5458042006050873a19a" "q-288816/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-288816/selection/3de79bddf6f841c5881cdae443d3aaa683eb5842e99646c5b7e2837e89382703" "q-288816/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-288816" :question/id #uuid "a3e9b421-3e24-576c-915f-687f04eee71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-288816" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-17x-30=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=6,\\,b=-17,\\,c=-30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-30)}{6}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-288816/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-288816"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-112960/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "5b605b48-1f56-5b52-a0a4-da41b6e648f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112960/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "10d7958e-1868-5d93-8a9d-23272d7e1433" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112960/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer/id #uuid "8b882184-5e97-5056-9ec0-a1434002a13a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112960/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "b7221697-c245-573c-be7d-652163f6e37f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112960/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "fa815d57-dd6d-544f-bee3-ecf188611a16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112960/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6be6948b-0bbe-50e4-8e11-a063db8462a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-112960/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-112960/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-112960/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" "q-112960/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" "q-112960/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-112960/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-112960" :question/id #uuid "6369285e-2dff-5b5c-a6d1-64b6011c7055" :question/math-academy-id "q-112960" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{3}-x-2,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-112960/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112960"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-319506/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" :answer/id #uuid "4b5b2859-3640-5b81-8651-2225339b75bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-319506/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "078ceede-3c63-5d57-a57a-728736efe9bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-319506/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf" :answer-field/choices ["q-319506/field-1/d569d64e1b94dabdc9f89c1070c848c6c96526a20f320d6b4971ae3a32604bcf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-319506" :question/id #uuid "54147b52-7d93-5805-b5d5-f24fa1587a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-319506" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-3+3\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947117/assets/d00bb449e3525bed0543e690ef41895da5ad9bd28632f127aa7d8e5876ce7deb.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-319506/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-319506"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-148300"] :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x+k}{9-x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y+k}{9-y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{y+k}{9-y} \\\\ x(9-y) & =y+k \\\\ 9x-xy & =y+k \\\\ 9x-k & =xy+y \\\\ 9x-k & =y(x+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{9x-k}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9x-k}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
